This covers the selection and arrangement of the records, and the text the project
wrote itself (summaries, notes, dating bases, readings and the like). It does NOT
cover third-party text. Where a row quotes a translation or an original-language
edition (passages.quote_translation, passages.quote_original), that text stays under
the licence of the source the row names (passages.translation_source_id,
passages.original_source_id, passages.source_id -> sources.licence), and section 2
applies to it.

2. Third-party sources

sources.csv records, for every source, one licence from a closed vocabulary:
public domain, CC0, CC BY 4.0, CC BY 3.0, CC BY-SA 4.0, CC BY-SA 3.0, copyright,
unconfirmed. The value is the most restrictive established reading across the
jurisdictions the project serves; per-jurisdiction detail is in sources.licence_note.
A passage is quoted verbatim only from a source whose licence is one of the first six.
Sources marked 'copyright' or 'unconfirmed' are cited and paraphrased, never quoted at
length. Share-alike (CC BY-SA) constrains what a reuser of that quoted text must do
downstream; it does not alter the licence of the rest of the dataset.

